{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nCloudWatch alarm `rebar-bridge-run-failures` entered ALARM at 2026-11-03 11:41 PST. Scheduled `reconcile-bridge` runs 41693706227 (10:25 PST), 41703055677 (11:25 PST) and 41712353911 (12:24 PST) all failed identically; the 09:24 PST run and earlier passed. Each failing run dies in `src/rebar/reconciler/_ref_lock.py` `_read_blob_bytes`: `git cat-file blob 36823c918daf:lock.json` exceeds `_LOCAL_TIMEOUT_SECS` (5.0 s), raises `RefLockTimeoutError`, and the pass lock fails CLOSED (`ReconcileLockError: cannot determine pass-lock state`), so no reconcile pass runs. The lock itself is released (released=True, fence 5, written 09:42:30 PST).\n\n## Root cause (one experiment so far)\nThe bridge workflow checks out with `filter: blob:none`, and every ref-lock fetch of `refs/reconciler/*` (the workflow's \"Sync reconciler lock refs\" step and `_fetch_ref`) inherits that partial-clone filter, so the payload blob is NOT local. `cat-file blob` therefore performs a lazy promisor fetch over the network, but ADR 0121 §10 (consolidating ADR 0031) budgets it as a LOCAL op (5 s; remote push/fetch get 30 s). In a fresh blobless clone on the operator host the first read of that exact blob took 4.44 s and the cached repeat 0.02 s, so a slower runner crosses 5 s. Unchecked: why it began at 10:25 PST and not earlier.\n\n## Approach\nClassify the payload read by what it may do, not where the object usually lives: in a partial clone a blob read can lazily fetch, so `_read_blob_bytes` uses `_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_SECS` for both `cat-file blob` calls. `_object_type` (`cat-file -t` on the commit, which blob:none clones carry) stays on the local budget. This is portable (no CI-specific step; any partial clone benefits) and adds no mechanism. Fail-closed semantics are unchanged: a 30 s timeout still raises `RefLockTimeoutError` and is treated as HELD. ADR 0121 §10 (\"Fail-closed reads and bounded timeouts\", the authoritative consolidated record) is amended to say payload blob reads take the remote budget because a partial clone may fetch them lazily. The archived ADR 0031 is kept verbatim as the evidence record and is not edited.\n\n## Files\n- `src/rebar/reconciler/_ref_lock.py` — `_read_blob_bytes` timeouts.\n- `docs/adr/0121-reconciler-architecture-consolidated-record.md` — §10 timeout sentence amendment.\n- `tests/unit/reconciler/state/test_ref_lock_blob_read_budget.py` — new test.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `_read_blob_bytes` passes `_REMOTE_TIMEOUT_SECS` to both `cat-file blob` reads (legacy blob and commit payload); a new unit test recording the timeout each `_git` call receives fails before the change and passes after.\n- [ ] `_object_type` still passes `_LOCAL_TIMEOUT_SECS`, asserted by the same test.\n- [ ] A new test builds a real `--filter=blob:none` clone of a local bare repo holding a `refs/reconciler/lock` commit and shows `read()` returns the released lock state, with the payload blob absent locally before the read.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0121-reconciler-architecture-consolidated-record.md` §10 states that payload blob reads use the 30 s remote budget because a partial clone may fetch them lazily; `git diff origin/main -- docs/adr/0031-reconciler-ref-lock.md` is empty.\n- [ ] `make lint` exits 0.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` exits 0.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/reconciler/` exits 0.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The first scheduled `reconcile-bridge` run after the merge succeeds and publishes `bridge_run_failures=0`.\n  provenance: production-equivalent / live-call\n- [ ] [non-codebase] `rebar-bridge-run-failures` returns to OK.\n  provenance: production-equivalent / live-call"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793741919566671001,"uuid":"1f7b2c51-9aa2-416e-9438-fa1d134123c4"}